course: dictionary
lesson: 4144
title: Glossy things and glossy looks
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Glossy means smooth and shiny, so light bounces off it like a mirror. Look at the context in «Tempering means warming and cooling melted chocolate in a careful pattern so the fat sets in neat tiny crystals that make the chocolate glossy and make it snap cleanly.» The words neat and snap cleanly show the chocolate is well made, and glossy describes how it looks.
explain: Glossy can describe many things: glossy hair, a glossy coat, glossy paint or glossy paper. When you meet it, try swapping in shiny. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
explain: Glossy can also mean looking attractive on the outside, sometimes hiding something less good. Glossy adverts and magazines are often made to look perfect, so ask yourself what is real and what is only pretty.
fact: Glossy means smooth and shiny, so it reflects light.
fact: A glossy magazine or photo is printed on shiny paper and often looks expensive.
fact: «Hóngshāoròu» is red-braised pork belly, sweet and glossy.
fact: In science, a glossy surface reflects light like a mirror, while a matt surface scatters it.
fact: Glossy can also mean looking attractive on the outside, sometimes hiding something less good.
exercise: Copy the word that means smooth and shiny: «The horse's glossy coat was brushed until it shone.» | until | horse's | *glossy | brushed
exercise: «The tempered chocolate was glossy and snapped cleanly.» What does glossy mean here? | *Smooth and shiny, reflecting light | Cold and hard like ice | Dull and rough | Soft and fluffy like a cloud
exercise: Mia says a glossy magazine is printed on cardboard. Is she right? | Yes, cardboard is always shiny | Yes, it is printed on wet sand | *No, it is printed on shiny paper | No, magazines are never printed
test: Why might a glossy advert hide something less good? | Because adverts are printed on shiny paper, so they must be real | Because adverts are always true | *Because it is made to look perfect | Because it is a very old photo
test: «The glossy paint dried quickly on the door.» What does glossy suggest? | *Smooth and shiny | Dusty and dull | Rough and bumpy | Wet and muddy
exam: Which sentence uses glossy to mean looking attractive but possibly hiding something? | The glossy cat purred on the warm mat. | *The glossy advert showed a perfect smile, but the product was broken. | The glossy paper was thin and cheap. | The glossy leaves reflected the sun like a mirror.
